  "answer_core": "Do not confront the partner, investigate privately, access systems unusually, or pick a reporting channel yourself. Tonight, write down exactly what you saw, preserve your own notes, tell your spouse in broad strokes, and contact two or three whistleblower attorneys.\n\nYour first move is independent counsel first, not GC-first by default and not a self-chosen audit committee or external report. Let counsel review the facts and decide whether GC, the audit committee, regulators, or another path is safest. During intake, ask practical questions that test whether the attorney has a reflexive channel preference: when have they advised internal-first, audit-committee-first, or external-first, and why?\n\nAttend Wednesday normally and avoid anything that looks like a sudden change in behavior. If the partner raises the encounter, keep the response narrow: do not deny, elaborate, confront, investigate, or be alone with the partner if avoidable. This is high risk, so do not let fear push you into filing before evidence review, but do not let fear make you do nothing either. Get counsel involved quickly and keep the first moves reversible.",
